versus individuated disease referents (generic vs. instance reference)\n- **Confusion Trigger:** Bare or lightly modified category-level clinical nouns with no proper-name, eponym, acronym, or gene anchor (e.g., \"vasculitis,\" \"arthritis,\" \"cutaneous vasculitis\"), plus plural morphology on a deficiency term (\"PAH deficiencies\"). The model reads these lexical forms as class labels, even when the mention refers to one specific condition in the document.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** TP SpecificDisease mentions name one disease entity through an anchor (\"Coats disease,\" \"SLS,\" \"G6PD deficiency\"). The TP DiseaseClass \"mental retardation\" is an unanchored term in a syndrome-level definition, so unanchored terms are normally DiseaseClass. The discrepancies differ from that TP in referent, not in form: they describe one patient's diagnosis.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Use an individuated-referent test, with names as the easy proxy.\n  1. **Anchored mention → SpecificDisease.** If the mention contains an eponym, acronym, gene symbol, or enzyme-defined deficiency name, label it SpecificDisease, even if the head is plural (\"PAH deficiencies\"). Plurality does not convert a named disease into a class.\n  2. **Unanchored mention in a patient-level frame → SpecificDisease.** If an unanchored clinical term denotes the individuated diagnosis or presenting condition of a described patient (\"a patient presenting with fever, arthritis, and cutaneous vasculitis\"; \"chronic meningococcemia presenting as vasculitis\"), label it SpecificDisease.\n  3. **Unanchored mention in a generic frame → DiseaseClass.** If it appears in a syndrome-level description (\"characterized by X\") or a generic differential (\"other causes of X\"), label it DiseaseClass.\n  \n  Example 5 falls under rule 3, so its gold label should be reviewed.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "s